              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Kannon K. Shanmugam (Petitioner), according to Kannon K. Shanmugam (Petitioner), the Armed Career Criminal Act (ACCA) is a key federal tool for incapacitating serial offenders, and it defines the prior offenses that trigger its application partly based on the maximum terms of imprisonment for those offenses."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Government",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "5564e048b47ed164e40c24bf64812da3f87f7fb081171eeed2c5850164d16c12",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-5564e048b47e-0003-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 54,
              "start": 54
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Government, the United States argues that the ACCA\u2019s language permits only a single maximum term that applies to all offenders, not separate maxima for recidivists."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Kannon K. Shanmugam (Petitioner)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "5564e048b47ed164e40c24bf64812da3f87f7fb081171eeed2c5850164d16c12",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-5564e048b47e-0000-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 4,
              "start": 4
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Kannon K. Shanmugam (Petitioner), according to Kannon K. Shanmugam (Petitioner), the respondent contends that the ACCA\u2019s language allows alternative maximum terms for repeat offenders versus first\u2011time offenders."
      }
    ],
